-- title:
--   Theorem 3.14 — equivalent simply coloured graphons on $[0,1]$ have a common pullback
-- statement:
--   Let $(W,\Psi)$ and $(\widetilde W,\widetilde\Psi)$ be simply coloured graphons on $[0,1]$ with Lebesgue measure (each colouring is measurable for the σ-field $\mathcal F_W$, respectively $\mathcal F_{\widetilde W}$, generated by the sections of its graphon), and suppose $(W,\Psi)\cong(\widetilde W,\widetilde\Psi)$. Then there exist measure-preserving maps $\varphi,\tilde\varphi:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ with
--   $$
--   W\bigl(\varphi(x),\varphi(y)\bigr)=\widetilde W\bigl(\tilde\varphi(x),\tilde\varphi(y)\bigr)\ \text{ a.e. on }[0,1]^2,\qquad
--   \Psi(\varphi(x))=\widetilde\Psi(\tilde\varphi(x))\ \text{ a.e. on }[0,1].
--   $$
--
--   Together with Theorem 3.11 this gives a uniqueness result for mixed coloured graphons, in the class of simply coloured representatives.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The pullback is $(W,\Psi)^\varphi=(W^\varphi,\Psi\circ\varphi)$, so the conclusion is the pair of a.e. equalities above. The maps need not be bijective.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: [0, 1] with Lebesgue measure λ → unitInterval with volume and the Borel σ-field; reason: Remark 3.3: the Borel and Lebesgue σ-fields give the same results up to a.e. equality
--   - encoding: colourings Ψ : Ω → Δκ, colours [κ]; pure colourings χ : Ω → [κ] → Ψ : Ω → Fin κ → ℝ with measurable nonnegative coordinates summing to 1 (IsKColouring); pure χ measurable with the point-mass colouring ofColour χ; reason: the same objects (Definition 2.1, p.7), with colours numbered 0, …, κ − 1
--   - encoding: W₁ ≅ W₂ (Definition 2.6: one, and thus all, of the conditions of Lemma 2.5) → CEquiv: t(F, W₁) = t(F, W₂) for every coloured graph F; reason: condition (i) of Lemma 2.5, which Definition 2.6 lists as equivalent to the others
--   - encoding: (W, Ψ)^φ = (W̃, Ψ̃)^φ̃ a.e. → W ∘ (φ × φ) = W̃ ∘ (φ̃ × φ̃) a.e. on [0, 1]² and Ψ ∘ φ = Ψ̃ ∘ φ̃ a.e. on [0, 1]; reason: (W, Ψ)^φ = (W^φ, Ψ ∘ φ) (p.11) and a.e. equality of coloured graphons is a.e. equality of both components (Remark 3.3)
-- source:
--   Janson, Lo, McDiarmid, Skeja, Skerman, Graphon Colourings and Limits of Graph Partitions, arXiv:2610.38599 (held version, sha256 3d198813a65d248922299a3380e10f238dbc5dcd3be060f7810b462bd5d3d10f), Theorem 3.14, p.25 (PDF p.25)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GraphonCheeger_Supplement_homDens
import Definitions.Def_GraphonCheeger_Supplement_sigmaW

open MeasureTheory

namespace GraphonCheeger.Supplement

/-- Theorem 3.14, p.25 (Janson, Lo, McDiarmid, Skeja, Skerman, *Graphon Colourings and Limits of Graph Partitions*,
arXiv:2610.38599 (held version, sha256 3d198813…)): let `(W, Ψ)` and `(W̃, Ψ̃)` be simply coloured graphons on `[0, 1]` with
`(W, Ψ) ≅ (W̃, Ψ̃)`. Then there exist measure-preserving maps `φ, φ̃ : [0, 1] → [0, 1]` with
`(W, Ψ)^φ = (W̃, Ψ̃)^φ̃` a.e.

**Formalization Note.** `[0, 1]` carries Lebesgue measure. Simply coloured: `Ψ` is
`F_W`-measurable (Def. 3.9, `IsSimpleColouring`). The pullback is `(W, Ψ)^φ = (W^φ, Ψ ∘ φ)`, so the
conclusion is `W^φ = W̃^φ̃` a.e. on `[0, 1]²` and `Ψ ∘ φ = Ψ̃ ∘ φ̃` a.e. on `[0, 1]`. The maps need
not be bijective. -/
theorem theorem_3_14 {κ : ℕ} (W W' : unitInterval → unitInterval → ℝ) (hW : IsGraphon W)
    (hW' : IsGraphon W') (Ψ Ψ' : unitInterval → Fin κ → ℝ) (hΨ : IsKColouring Ψ)
    (hΨ' : IsKColouring Ψ') (hs : IsSimpleColouring W Ψ) (hs' : IsSimpleColouring W' Ψ')
    (hequiv : CEquiv volume W Ψ volume W' Ψ') :
    ∃ φ φ' : unitInterval → unitInterval,
      MeasurePreserving φ volume volume ∧ MeasurePreserving φ' volume volume ∧
      (∀ᵐ p ∂((volume : Measure unitInterval).prod volume),
        W (φ p.1) (φ p.2) = W' (φ' p.1) (φ' p.2)) ∧
      (∀ᵐ x ∂(volume : Measure unitInterval), Ψ (φ x) = Ψ' (φ' x)) := by sorry

end GraphonCheeger.Supplement
